issue_comments.body:
Review follow-up pushed as **1cdd44d4a** (one commit on top of 7a80bc6a4):

* the `"hf"` gate's two prose sites state the rule the code enforces -- every element within `_HF_ULP_CAP` (6) io ulps at the larger rotation operand's magnitude, with its derivation -- instead of the retired "four io ulps" of the result;
* the step kernel's appended constexpr is `ring_write` end to end (the public key's name; the per-row store predicate is now `ring_store`) -- a rename, no expression changed;
* `TOP_K_MAX == cudnn.QSA.selection.QSA_TOP_K` pinned in the canonical-list tests;
* the gather docstring names the served sequence's partial-group zero row and the report precedence (dead > short > over > served; short / over disjoint under `T <= POOL x NB`);
* the two code-scanning notes in `test_qsa_kbar_gather.py` fixed.

From this PR's CI run: the sm90 job's single red, `test_hf_rounding_is_the_hf_oracles_rounding_points_and_single_is_not[pool1_queries-f16]` (one f16 element of 524288 two RESULT ulps off, 2.4e-7, at a rotated dim), is the `"single"` arm's yardstick, not the kernel -- the same cancelling-sum lesson the `"hf"` gate already applies -- and is fixed in the same commit: the one-ulp budget is measured at the larger rotation operand's magnitude (the count stays one ulp). The sm107 job of that run ran all six kernel-level modules on cc 10.7: 515 passed, 0 skipped. The default arms of the three modified kernels are bitwise `develop` on 26 outputs (A100; the cc 10.7 repeat is the one unchecked box in the body). The other reds of that run (BSA fp8 metadata, trimul span, norm lowering on sm100) are pre-existing and outside this diff.

Title shortened per review; body updated with the evidence above.
